produces the identical NaN **bit pattern** `0xFFC00000` on both sides.\n- ABI: garbage in the register byte above the 3-byte struct leaks into neither implementation (500k comparisons).\n\n**Phase D — all 4 configurations** (default / `--no-default-features` × debug / release) pass 26/26 via `./verify_all.sh`. Testing both profiles mattered here: debug is `opt-level=0` and release `opt-level=3`, genuinely different float codegen.\n\nTwo things worth flagging about the process:\n\n1. **My first test run \"failed\" on my own metric, not on a real bug.** I had asserted ≥60 of 64 rows exercise both swap directions; 57 did. The cause was inherent — a row pairing an all-dark operand against an all-bright one can only produce one ordering. Since each row already checks both `(a,b)` and `(b,a)`, both sides *were* covered and my counter was just tracking one direction. I fixed the metric and tightened the assertion to a strict 64/64 rather than lowering the bar.\n\n2. **I mutation-tested the suite to prove it isn't vacuous.** Injecting f64 accumulation (M1) and a single extra f32 rounding in the `pow` argument (M2) each caused **10 tests to fail** — so the suite really is sensitive to the sub-ULP precision slips this kind of translation is prone to. A `cp` restore silently failed mid-way (`$TMPDIR` is per-invocation here, so my backup vanished), briefly stacking both mutations; I caught it, restored both lines exactly, and confirmed 144 lines with no residue before the final run.\n\n`c_src/` was not modified — the only addition is the `build/` directory the task's own instructions call for."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1117,"cache_read_input_tokens":100301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1117,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4aeb89ce-3f40-4d71-bc31-50d5f7539e91","u